{
  "APPELLANT’S OPENING BRIEF": ["heading", "framing"],
  "IN THE COURT OF APPEAL OF THE STATE OF CALIFORNIA": ["other", "framing"],
  "FIFTH APPELLATE DISTRICT": ["other", "framing"],
  "MARIA DELGADO, Plaintiff and Appellant,": ["other", "framing"],
  "v.": ["other", "framing"],
  "FRESHWAY MARKETS, INC., Defendant and Respondent.": ["other", "framing"],
  "Case No. F087412": ["other", "framing"],
  "Appeal from the Superior Court of Fresno County, No. 22CECG01957, Hon. Thomas R. Ellery, Judge": [
    "other",
    "framing"
  ],
  "INTRODUCTION": ["heading", "framing"],
  "Maria Delgado fell in a FreshWay Markets store when she stepped in a pool of liquid laundry detergent that, by FreshWay’s own account, no employee had looked for in nearly an hour.": [
    "facts",
    "client-fact"
  ],
  "The trial court granted summary judgment because Delgado could not say exactly how long the detergent had been on the floor.": [
    "facts",
    "client-fact"
  ],
  "But as the Supreme Court made clear in Ortega, California law has never required a plaintiff to time a spill with a stopwatch.": [
    "rule",
    "law",
    "explanation"
  ],
  "The judgment should be reversed.": ["conclusion", "conclusion"],
  "STATEMENT OF THE CASE": ["heading", "framing"],
  "Delgado sued FreshWay for negligence and premises liability on April 11, 2022. (1 CT 1-9.)": [
    "facts",
    "client-fact"
  ],
  "FreshWay moved for summary judgment, arguing that Delgado had no evidence that it knew or should have known of the spill. (1 CT 24-41.)": [
    "facts",
    "client-fact"
  ],
  "Delgado opposed the motion with the declaration of a retail-safety expert, Dr. Alan Whitcomb. (2 CT 301-322.)": [
    "facts",
    "client-fact"
  ],
  "The trial court sustained FreshWay’s objections to paragraphs 6 through 11 of the Whitcomb declaration, granted the motion, and entered judgment on September 8, 2023. (3 CT 611-618; RT 14:2-22.)": [
    "facts",
    "client-fact"
  ],
  "Delgado timely appealed. (3 CT 624.)": ["facts", "client-fact"],
  "STATEMENT OF FACTS": ["heading", "framing"],
  "On the afternoon of February 2, 2021, Delgado was shopping in aisle 7 of FreshWay’s store on Shaw Avenue in Fresno. (1 CT 52, 58.)": [
    "facts",
    "client-fact"
  ],
  "As she turned toward the end cap, her right foot slid on a clear blue liquid, and she fell onto her left hip. (1 CT 53; 2 CT 345.)": [
    "facts",
    "client-fact"
  ],
  "The liquid was later identified as laundry detergent from a 150-oz. jug on the bottom shelf, which had a cracked cap. (2 CT 346-347.)": [
    "facts",
    "client-fact"
  ],
  "FreshWay’s written safety policy requires an employee to walk every aisle and complete a “floor sweep” log at least once every 30 minutes. (2 CT 360.)": [
    "facts",
    "client-fact"
  ],
  "The log for February 2 shows that aisle 7 was last swept at 2:05 p.m.; Delgado fell at 3:01 p.m. (2 CT 362; RT 9:14-18.)": [
    "facts",
    "client-fact"
  ],
  "The assistant manager, Kevin Ostrander, testified that the 2:35 p.m. sweep “probably just didn’t get done” because the store was short-staffed that day. (2 CT 371 [Ostrander depo. at 44:3-19].)": [
    "facts",
    "client-fact"
  ],
  "He agreed that a leaking jug of detergent “would make a puddle that size in maybe twenty minutes, maybe more.” (2 CT 373.)": [
    "facts",
    "client-fact"
  ],
  "Dr. Whitcomb, a certified safety professional who has audited more than 200 grocery stores, reviewed the sweep logs and photographs. (2 CT 301-304.)": [
    "facts",
    "client-fact"
  ],
  "He stated that a puddle of the size shown in the photographs, roughly 30 inches across, would take at least 25 minutes to form from a cracked cap. (2 CT 306, ¶ 8.)": [
    "facts",
    "client-fact"
  ],
  "STANDARD OF REVIEW": ["heading", "framing"],
  "A trial court properly grants summary judgment only when “all the papers submitted show that there is no triable issue as to any material fact and that the moving party is entitled to a judgment as a matter of law.” Cal. Civ. Proc. Code § 437c(c).": [
    "rule",
    "law"
  ],
  "This court reviews the grant of summary judgment de novo. Aguilar v. Atl. Richfield Co., 25 Cal. 4th 826, 860, 24 P.3d 493, 107 Cal. Rptr. 2d 841 (2001).": [
    "rule",
    "law"
  ],
  "It considers all the evidence the parties submitted, except evidence to which objections were properly sustained, and views that evidence and the reasonable inferences from it in the light most favorable to the opposing party. Id. at 843; Saelzler v. Advanced Grp. 400, 25 Cal. 4th 763, 768, 23 P.3d 1143, 107 Cal. Rptr. 2d 617 (2001).": [
    "rule",
    "law"
  ],
  "Rulings on evidentiary objections made in connection with a summary judgment motion are generally reviewed for abuse of discretion, although the Supreme Court has left open whether a de novo standard applies. See Reid v. Google, Inc., 50 Cal. 4th 512, 535, 235 P.3d 988, 113 Cal. Rptr. 3d 327 (2010).": [
    "rule",
    "law"
  ],
  "ARGUMENT": ["heading", "framing"],
  "I. THE TRIAL COURT ERRED IN GRANTING SUMMARY JUDGMENT BECAUSE A TRIABLE ISSUE EXISTS ON CONSTRUCTIVE NOTICE": [
    "heading",
    "conclusion"
  ],
  "A store owner must use ordinary care to keep its premises reasonably safe for customers. Cal. Civ. Code § 1714(a); Ortega v. Kmart Corp., 26 Cal. 4th 1200, 1205, 36 P.3d 11, 114 Cal. Rptr. 2d 470 (2001).": [
    "rule",
    "law"
  ],
  "To hold an owner liable for a dangerous condition it did not create, the plaintiff must show that the owner had actual or constructive knowledge of the condition in time to correct it. Ortega, 26 Cal. 4th at 1206.": [
    "rule",
    "law"
  ],
  "FreshWay’s motion rested entirely on the absence of direct evidence of how long the detergent was on the floor. (1 CT 31-33.)": [
    "counter",
    "client-fact"
  ],
  "That is not the law, and FreshWay’s own records supply the inference the trial court found missing.": [
    "counter",
    "application"
  ],
  "A. Evidence That FreshWay Skipped a Required Inspection Permits an Inference That a Reasonable Inspection Would Have Revealed the Spill": [
    "heading",
    "conclusion"
  ],
  "In Ortega, the Supreme Court rejected the argument that a plaintiff must prove exactly how long a hazard existed. Id. at 1210–11.": [
    "explanation",
    "precedent"
  ],
  "The court explained that “[w]hether a dangerous condition has existed long enough for a reasonably prudent person to have discovered it is a question of fact for the jury.” Id. at 1207 (internal quotation marks omitted).": [
    "explanation",
    "precedent"
  ],
  "It added that a plaintiff may raise that inference “by showing that the site had not been inspected within a reasonable period of time.” Id. at 1210.": [
    "explanation",
    "precedent"
  ],
  "Courts have applied the same rule to grocery spills. See, e.g., Hatfield v. Levy Bros., 18 Cal. 2d 798, 806, 117 P.2d 841 (1941); Louie v. Hagstrom’s Food Stores, Inc., 81 Cal. App. 2d 601, 608, 184 P.2d 708 (1947).": [
    "explanation",
    "law"
  ],
  "Here, FreshWay’s policy required a sweep of aisle 7 every 30 minutes, and its log shows that none occurred for 56 minutes before Delgado fell. (2 CT 360, 362.)": [
    "application",
    "client-fact"
  ],
  "Like the store in Ortega, FreshWay cannot rely on its own failure to inspect to defeat the inference of notice. See Ortega, 26 Cal. 4th at 1210.": [
    "application",
    "application"
  ],
  "A jury could reasonably find that an employee walking the aisle at 2:35 p.m. would have seen a 30-inch blue puddle and cleaned it up before 3:01 p.m.": [
    "application",
    "application"
  ],
  "The trial court therefore erred in finding no triable issue on constructive notice.": [
    "conclusion",
    "conclusion"
  ],
  "B. The Trial Court Improperly Weighed the Evidence Instead of Drawing Inferences in Delgado’s Favor": [
    "heading",
    "conclusion"
  ],
  "On summary judgment, a court may not weigh the evidence or decide which competing inference is more persuasive. Aguilar, 25 Cal. 4th at 856.": [
    "rule",
    "law"
  ],
  "Instead, it must deny the motion if the evidence would allow a reasonable trier of fact to find the contested fact in favor of the opposing party. Id. at 850; see Saelzler, 25 Cal. 4th at 781 (citing Aguilar, 25 Cal. 4th at 850).": [
    "rule",
    "law"
  ],
  "The trial court did the opposite.": ["application", "application"],
  "It reasoned that the detergent “could just as easily have spilled thirty seconds before plaintiff arrived.” (RT 12:3-9.)": [
    "application",
    "client-fact"
  ],
  "That speculation favors the moving party, and it ignores Ostrander’s own estimate that a puddle of that size takes about twenty minutes to form. (2 CT 373.)": [
    "application",
    "application"
  ],
  "In Saelzler, by contrast, the plaintiff offered nothing but speculation about who attacked her and how; here the record contains the defendant’s own admissions. See Saelzler, supra, at 775–76.": [
    "application",
    "application",
    "explanation"
  ],
  "II. THE TRIAL COURT ABUSED ITS DISCRETION IN EXCLUDING THE WHITCOMB DECLARATION": [
    "heading",
    "conclusion"
  ],
  "An expert may base an opinion on matter “of a type that reasonably may be relied upon by an expert in forming an opinion upon the subject.” Cal. Evid. Code § 801(b).": [
    "rule",
    "law"
  ],
  "The trial court acts as a gatekeeper, but its role is limited to excluding opinion that is speculative or unsupported by the material on which it relies. Sargon Enters., Inc. v. Univ. of S. Cal., 55 Cal. 4th 747, 771–72, 288 P.3d 1237, 149 Cal. Rptr. 3d 614 (2012).": [
    "rule",
    "law"
  ],
  "The court “does not resolve scientific controversies” and may not exclude an opinion merely because it disagrees with the expert’s conclusion. Id. at 772.": [
    "rule",
    "law"
  ],
  "On summary judgment, moreover, an opposing party’s expert declaration is liberally construed in that party’s favor. Fernandez v. Alexander, 31 Cal. App. 5th 770, 779, 243 Cal. Rptr. 3d 110 (2019).": [
    "rule",
    "law"
  ],
  "Dr. Whitcomb based his timing estimate on the photographs, the jug’s capacity, and his own testing of similar caps. (2 CT 305-307.)": [
    "application",
    "client-fact"
  ],
  "That is precisely the kind of reasoned explanation Sargon requires. Sargon, 55 Cal. 4th at 771.": [
    "application",
    "application"
  ],
  "Because the excluded paragraphs bore directly on how long the spill existed, the error was prejudicial. See Cal. Const. art. VI, § 13.": [
    "conclusion",
    "conclusion"
  ],
  "CONCLUSION": ["heading", "framing"],
  "Because a jury could find that FreshWay had constructive notice of the spill, and because the trial court erroneously excluded the evidence that most directly showed it, the judgment should be reversed.": [
    "conclusion",
    "conclusion"
  ],
  "Delgado respectfully requests that this court reverse the judgment and direct the trial court to vacate its order granting summary judgment and enter a new order denying the motion.": [
    "conclusion",
    "conclusion"
  ]
}
